## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Probability)
## DBchapter(Continuous distributions)
## DBsection(Application of a normal distribution)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(HeightWeight)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('3')

## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'normal distribution', 'z score')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

@question = (
    "About what percent of men are taller than 74?",
    "Between what approximate heights do the middle 95 percent of men fall?",
    "About what percent of men are shorter than 66.5 inches?",
    "About what percent of men are taller than 69 inches?",
    "About what percent of men are between 69 and 74 inches?",
    "About what percent of men are between 64 and 66.5 inches?",
    "Fill in the blank: About 2.5 percent of all men are shorter than ________."
);

@ans = (
    PGML('[_]{2.5} %'),  PGML('[_]{64} to [_]{74} inches'),
    PGML('[_]{16} %'),   PGML('[_]{50} %'),
    PGML('[_]{47.5} %'), PGML('[_]{13.5} %'),
    PGML('[_]{64} in')
);

@slice = random_subset(3, 0 .. 6);

## @rb = map { RadioButtons([ $opts[$_], 'None of these' ], 0, labels => 'ABC') }
## @slice;

BEGIN_PGML
The distribution of heights of adult men in the U.S. is approximately normal with mean 69 inches and 
standard deviation 2.5 inches.  Use what you know about a normal distribution and the 68-95-99.7 
rule to answer the questions below. 

a) [$question[$slice[0]]] 

    [$ans[$slice[0]]]*

b) [$question[$slice[1]]]

    [$ans[$slice[1]]]*

c) [$question[$slice[2]]]

    [$ans[$slice[2]]]*

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For the 68-95-99.7 percent rule recall that 

- 68% fall within 1 standard deviation of the mean
- 95% fall within 2 standard deviations of the mean
- 99.7% fall within 3 standard deviations of the mean.

Since the distribution is symmetric, this also means that half of 95%-68% = 13.5% fall between 1 and 2 standard devations of the mean (one sided).  There are similar calculations that can be done. 

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.